              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Men have larger, more prominent Adam\u2019s apples due to a combination of testosterone and anatomical structure. \n\nThe medical term for the Adam\u2019s apple is the **laryngeal prominence**. It is not actually a separate organ or bone, but rather a protrusion of the **thyroid cartilage**, a shield-like structure that wraps around and protects the larynx (voice box) and vocal cords. \n\nThis feature is more prominent in men for two primary reasons:\n\n### 1. The Role of Testosterone and Puberty\nBefore puberty, boys and girls have similarly sized voice boxes. However, once puberty begins, boys experience a massive surge in the hormone **testosterone**. \n\nTestosterone causes the larynx to grow significantly larger and faster in males. As the larynx grows, the vocal cords inside it stretch, becoming longer and thicker. This is what causes a male's voice to \"break\" and drop to a deeper pitch. To protect this newly enlarged voice box, the surrounding thyroid cartilage must also grow much larger, pushing outward against the skin.\n\n### 2. The Angle of the Cartilage\nThe thyroid cartilage is made up of two plates (called laminae) that meet in the middle of the throat. The angle at which these two plates fuse determines how much the bump sticks out:\n* **In males:** The plates meet at a sharp, narrow angle of about **90 degrees**. This creates a sharp \"V\" shape that protrudes forward prominently.\n* **In females:** The plates meet at a wider, more obtuse angle of about **120 degrees**. Because the angle is wider, the cartilage lies flatter against the neck and is much less noticeable.\n\n### Do women have Adam's apples?\nYes, **everyone has one**. Females require a larynx to speak, and they also have thyroid cartilage to protect it. However, because females do not experience the same surge of testosterone during puberty, their voice boxes remain smaller, their vocal cords remain shorter (resulting in a higher voice), and the cartilage remains flatter and smaller. \n\nOccasionally, some women can have a visible Adam's apple. This can be due to natural genetic variation, a higher-than-average level of testosterone during puberty, or simply having a very slender neck or low body fat, which makes the cartilage more visible.",
